description:
crate-jmap-sharing-server/src/principal.rs:121-123 and memory.rs:498-499 / memory.rs:551-552

The crate has settled on the convention that serde_json::to_value on a derive-Serialize plain-data type is provably infallible, and uses .expect(...) with a justifying comment to express that. principal.rs:121-123:

    serde_json::to_value(&created_obj)
        .expect('derive(Serialize) on plain data is infallible'),

and helpers.rs:18-20:

    pub(crate) fn set_error_value(e: &crate::backend::SetError) -> serde_json::Value {
        serde_json::to_value(e).expect('derive(Serialize) on plain data is infallible')
    }

The same shape appears at memory.rs:498-499 (create_object body) and memory.rs:551-552 (update_object body), but with map_err instead of expect:

    let mut val = serde_json::to_value(&obj)
        .map_err(|e| BackendSetError::Other(MemoryError(format!('serialize: {e}'))))?;
    ...
    let patch_val = serde_json::to_value(&patch)
        .map_err(|e| BackendSetError::Other(MemoryError(format!('serialize patch: {e}'))))?;

Both Principal and the SetObject::Patch associated type are derive-Serialize on plain data. The infallibility argument that justifies .expect at the handler site applies equally to the backend site. The map_err arm is dead code that the type checker preserves only because Result threading requires it.

Choose one idiom:

(a) Use .expect at all sites with the same justification comment. Consistent with the handler convention.
(b) Use map_err at all sites (preserves a runtime escape for a future serde_json bug). Consistent with the conservative defense-in-depth posture but contradicts the handler's commitment.

The current code has BOTH conventions in the same crate within ~400 lines. The mixing is the problem; either resolution is fine.

What goes wrong for callers when this stays as-is: a reader who finds memory.rs:498 first reads it as 'serialize can fail; the backend handles it' and writes their own backend with similar map_err. A reader who finds principal.rs:121 first reads it as 'serialize is infallible on plain-data structs; just .expect it' and writes their backend that way. Two writing styles, same crate, no documented rationale for the difference.
